concepts.content:
To model nonlinear functions, token nets use tokenwise operators, which apply a transformation to each token individually and independently. Given a nonlinear function $F_{\theta}: \mathbb{R}^N \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^N$, a tokenwise nonlinearity layer is expressed as:

$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{T}_{\texttt{out}}= 
        \begin{bmatrix}
        F_{\theta}(\mathbf{T}_{\texttt{in}}[0,:]) \\
        \vdots \\
        F_{\theta}(\mathbf{T}_{\texttt{in}}[N-1,:]) \\
        \end{bmatrix}\quad\quad \triangleleft \quad\text{per-token nonlinearity}
\end{aligned}$$

This generalizes pointwise nonlinearities in regular neural nets (like ReLU). A key difference is that while standard ReLUs have no learnable parameters, $F_{\theta}$ in token nets is often a multilayer perceptron (MLP) with its own learnable weights and biases, making these pointwise operations highly expressive.

