concepts.content:
A token-wise MLP applies the same MLP $F_{\theta}$ to each token in a sequence. The $F_{\theta}$ consists of linear layers and pointwise nonlinearities. Assuming no biases, the linear layers in $F_{\theta}$ have the form:
$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{t}_{\texttt{out}} &= \mathbf{W}\mathbf{t}_{\texttt{in}}
\end{aligned}$$
When applied to each token in the sequence, we have:
$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{T}_{\texttt{out}}&= \mathbf{T}_{\texttt{in}}\mathbf{W}^{\mathsf{T}}
\end{aligned}$$
This is identical to the query operation, meaning the linear layers of the token-wise MLP can all be written as convolutions with kernel size 1. Since the pointwise nonlinearities in the MLP are applied neuronwise, they function identically to the pointwise nonlinearity in CNNs. Therefore, a token-wise MLP is equivalent to a series of convolutions interleaved with neuronwise-nonlinearities, which is a CNN.

